    "body": "<p>A healthy coral reef is loud. Snapping shrimp produce sharp broadband snaps as their claws close, creating cavitation bubbles whose collapse makes the crackle that dominates many reef recordings; grazing fish scrape algae, urchins rasp, and reef fish grunt, pop, and croak, especially at dawn and dusk. Researchers have found that reef sound levels and complexity track reef health: degraded, overfished reefs are measurably quieter and less complex than protected, fish-rich reefs. Sound also guides young reef life: larvae of some fish and coral species orient toward reef sound when choosing where to settle, so a quiet reef may recruit fewer newcomers. Reef recordings therefore serve as a rapid health check for ecosystems that are hard to survey by eye, and playback of healthy reef sound is being tested as a way to encourage settlement on restoration sites. This record covers biophony, the reef animals, and geophony, the water and waves that carry their sounds.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: hydrophone surveys comparing protected and degraded reefs report higher sound levels and higher acoustic complexity at healthy sites, with snapping shrimp snaps as the dominant broadband source. Settlement experiments show larvae of several reef species responding to reef sound playbacks in laboratory and field trials, supporting sound&#x27;s role in habitat selection. Acoustic monitoring is now used alongside visual surveys in reef assessment programmes.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: if a healthy reef records 3000 snaps per minute and a degraded reef records 900 snaps per minute, the degraded reef&#x27;s snap rate is 900 / 3000 = 30.0 percent of the healthy rate, a reduction of 70.0 percent. The demo prints the healthy rate 3000, the degraded rate 900, the ratio 30.0 percent, and the reduction 70.0 percent.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony; geophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Published coral reef bioacoustics literature (snapping shrimp acoustics; reef health and acoustic complexity studies); figures as published October 2026</p>",
